.TH CHTTPSVR_STOP 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_stop \- close this server's listener
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpserver.h>

void chttpsvr_stop(chttpsvr srv);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Removes the listener socket registered by chttpsvr_start from the
shared reactor and closes it (unlinking the socket file first, for a
Unix domain socket listener).  Returns immediately.  The shared reactor
and all other registered listeners continue running unaffected.

Idempotent: calling chttpsvr_stop on a server that was never started,
or that was already stopped, is a no-op.

A silent no-op if srv is CHTTPSVR_INVALID or a stale/already-destroyed
handle, matching this function's existing "srv is NULL" behavior.

Connections already accepted before this call keep running; they are
not closed and may continue dispatching further requests through this
handle for as long as the client keeps the connection open, even
across a subsequent chttpsvr_start restart on the same handle.
__chttpsvr_destroy is the only call that waits for such in-flight
requests to fully drain (and closes every remaining idle connection)
before releasing the server's resources.

Safe to call with CHTTPSVR_INVALID (0).
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Synchronous and NOT async-signal-safe: unlike chttpsvr_engine_stop(),
do not call this function directly from a signal handler. It takes
internal locks that the interrupted thread could already be holding
(for example, inside chttpsvr_start() or chttpsvr_destroy()), which
can self-deadlock the calling thread. For a signal-driven shutdown
hook, use chttpsvr_engine_stop() instead; only that function is
documented as async-signal-safe, specifically because its
implementation defers all such work off of the interrupted thread.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_start (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_stop (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_wait (3)
